  "worked_solution": "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194531/assets/4531a531fb98f092751cec4552d6ead2bf828759842214f030618dba1ea85893.png)\n\n \n\nNow, let's look at each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n- Statement II is false, since the domain of the function $f(x)={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is true. For $x>0,$ the graph increases without a bound as $x$ increases to infinity.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true.",
